        "explanation": "The speaker asks for a specific piece of information: the current entry code for the side door. The reply \"It changed to four, one, nine, two on Monday\" delivers exactly that, giving the numbers and even noting when they were updated, which is why it is correct. \"The side door faces the river, so the view is nice\" latches onto the words \"side door\" but answers a question about location and scenery that nobody asked; the speaker wanted numbers, not a description of the building. \"Yes, coding class meets Tuesday nights in the lab\" reacts to the sound of the word \"code\" rather than its meaning here, since \"code\" in the question refers to a keypad combination, not to computer programming or a course schedule. \"I already returned that book to the front desk\" is impossible as a response, because nothing in the question mentioned a book or a loan; it answers a completely different conversation and leaves the request for the door code unaddressed.",
