        "image": "/img/listening/announcement/female-1.webp",
        "speakerGender": "female",
        "transcript": "Today I want to work through a question that sounds simple: why do some mountains stay white all year while others lose their snow every summer?\n\nLet's start with the basic physics. As you climb, air pressure drops, the air expands, and it cools. The usual rate is about six or seven degrees Celsius per kilometer of elevation. So a peak standing four kilometers above the surrounding plain is roughly twenty-six degrees colder at the top than at the base.\n\nBut temperature alone isn't the whole story. What actually matters is a balance: how much snow arrives each year versus how much melts or evaporates away. The elevation where those two quantities cancel out is called the snow line. Above it, snow survives the summer and piles up year after year. Below it, everything that falls in winter is gone by August.\n\nHere's the point students most often get wrong. They assume the snow line sits at the same altitude everywhere. It doesn't. Take a worked example. On an equatorial peak in East Africa, permanent snow begins around forty-eight hundred meters. Now move to southern Alaska, where mountains of very similar height carry ice starting near a thousand meters. Mountains of the same height, completely different snow lines, because latitude changes the amount of solar energy the surface receives.\n\nPrecipitation matters too. A dry, cold peak may sit above freezing thresholds and still show bare rock, simply because not enough snow ever arrives to build a lasting cover.\n\nSo think in terms of supply and removal, not height alone.",
